    $500 brief became a ranked Des Moines waterproofing company. Full story also on the
    <a href="/case-studies/dry-duck">Dry Duck case study</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The brief</h2>
  <p>
    An owner I already worked with on another business said, essentially: here’s about
    <strong>$500</strong>. Spin up a quick one-pager for a new waterproofing company. That was the
    whole mandate. Two months later we weren’t looking at a brochure. We were looking at a ranked
    local brand booking estimates.
  </p>

  <h2>Brand first: show the promise</h2>
  <p>
    We started with names and marks. “Dry Duck” stuck. Influenced by ideas from
    <em>Branded Not Blended</em> and StoryBrand, the goal was simple: <strong>show, don’t tell</strong>.
    Dry Duck stops moisture. So we built a character holding a shield against a tidal wave:
    the promise in one image, no speech required.
  </p>
  <p>
    Intuitive brands that emerge quickly often beat over-workshopped ones. This one felt obvious the
    moment it clicked, and it carried the whole site.
  </p>

  <h2>Website structure: StoryBrand + E-E-A-T</h2>
  <p>
    Google cares about experience, expertise, authority, and trust. Users care about whether you can
    help them in the next thirty seconds. So the homepage is built to hit both:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Hero loaded with trust and authority (most people never scroll).</li>
    <li>Services listed with clear descriptions when they wonder “do you do my job?”</li>
    <li>Service areas when they wonder “do you cover me?”</li>
    <li>Simple 1-2-3 process when they wonder “what happens if I call?”</li>
    <li>Risk / “what if it gets worse” framing instead of empty “why us” fluff.</li>
    <li>FAQs as expertise signals, then a final CTA.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Keywords come from Google Keyword Planner first (basement waterproofing was the head term),
    then UX frameworks, not the other way around. We put relevance in titles and headers. We don’t
    spam the body.
  </p>

  <h2>Local SEO: win the home city, then expand</h2>
  <p>
    Dry Duck serves a wide Iowa footprint, but the Google Business Profile lives in
    <strong>Des Moines</strong>. Trying to rank “all of Iowa” on day one confuses Google. Our
    approach:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Reinforce the true home location (Des Moines) on the site and profile.</li>
    <li>Use honest <strong>“we serve”</strong> language for surrounding cities, not fake “offices everywhere.”</li>
    <li>Build service-area pages that expand the circle after the core city is strong.</li>
    <li>Don’t explode the site with hundreds of thin AI pages on week one. Grow domain trust, then add.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Within roughly two months, heatmap-style rank tracking showed strong visibility across a wide
    radius for the main keyword (on the order of thousands of monthly searches in-market), with
    average positions in the top few results in key zones, and a clear next push into West Des
    Moines, Ankeny, Urbandale, and similar.
  </p>

  <h2>Reviews: velocity beats vanity totals</h2>
  <p>
    Dry Duck racked up dozens of reviews unusually fast (the owner already knew how to ask). That’s
    not typical, but the lesson is universal: <strong>recent review pace</strong> matters more than
    a dusty pile of old five-stars. Watch what top competitors earn per week. Beat that cadence.
  </p>

  <h2>Results while ads were still cold</h2>
  <p>
    Early on, paid ads weren’t the engine yet. Organic + profile + site structure were. We saw days
    with multiple estimates booked, and the owner reported roughly
    <strong>$80k in revenue within about three weeks</strong> of the first customers landing, the
    kind of signal that tells you the foundation is real.
  </p>

  <h2>Why we build with AI-assisted development</h2>
  <p>
    Same SEO standards. Completely different speed. Spinning a new service-area page, or changing a
    layout across dozens of pages, is painful in classic WordPress/page-builder land, waiting on
    loads, fighting templates, drifting meta tags. With a code-based site and AI-assisted workflows
    (Cursor / similar), those changes are minutes, and we can enforce an SEO checklist every time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Roughly speaking, it’s often an order of magnitude faster than even a well-optimized WordPress
    process, and design exploration gets faster too.
  </p>

  <h2>Takeaways for trade owners</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Start with Keyword Planner truth, not agency poetry.</li>
    <li>Make the hero earn trust immediately.</li>
    <li>Rank your real home city before you chase the whole state.</li>
    <li>Review velocity is a ranking and conversion weapon.</li>
    <li>Your website stack should make iteration cheap, because SEO is never “set and forget.”</li>
  </ul>
